N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: bar 1 E-G-A, E-G-A (eighth, eighth, quarter twice), the rising motif; bar 2 A-G-G-A and a quarter rest, the question ending high on la; bar 3 A-G-E, A-G-E marked staccato, the motif turned upside down; bar 4 G-E-E-E and a rest, the answer ending on mi; bar 5 E-G-A, G-A-A, the rising motif pushed higher; bar 6 A (accented)-G-A-A and a rest, the second question; bar 7 A-G-E, G-E-E, smooth; bar 8 G-G-E and a rest, the final answer on mi. Every bar totals four quarter beats; the only pitches are E4, G4 and A4; range E4-A4; the largest leap is the perfect fourth between E and A where the three-note motif restarts (inside bars 1, 3 and 5) or a phrase begins after a rest; durations are eighths, quarters and quarter rests only. Originality: rising and falling mi-so-la shapes are common property; the tune was checked against "Sodade" (melody and lyric), "Bounce High, Bounce Low" (which also sets high and low words but on a so-la-so-mi shape this tune does not use), "Rain, Rain, Go Away" and "Lucy Locket" and shares no phrase with them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether 76 is slow enough to feel gentle without young singers losing the line, whether children hear the staccato of bar 3 and the accent of bar 6 as different from the rest, and whether the "rise instead" choice is fun or confusing have not been tested. The new title needs the reviewer's agreement. A Cabo Verdean music educator, a classroom trial and a second score reader remain required.
